  * sweetbattery=1: read-only PM6150 polling every 30 seconds; voltage-derived
  * SOC is an UNCALIBRATED estimate, not a replacement for the Linux QG algo.
  * See report.md for binary evidence, register provenance and integration.
+ *
+ * Galaxy S9 (the default on this board, boot-arg sweetbattery=0 for the
+ * static fallback): the battery is measured by the MAX77705's fuel gauge and
+ * charged by its charger, both on the bit-banged bus of
+ * bsd/dev/sweet_max77705.c, polled every 10 s. Read only unless asked:
+ *   fuel gauge 0x36 (ModelGauge m5, drivers/battery_v2/max77705_fuelgauge.c):
+ *     RepSOC 0x06 (1/256 %), VCell 0x09 (78.125 uV), Current 0x0A and
+ *     AvgCurrent 0x0B (1.5625 uV / 5 mOhm = 312.5 uA, positive = charging),
+ *     RepCap 0x05 and FullCapRep 0x35 (1 mAh with the 5 mOhm sense resistor,
+ *     DT fg_resistor = 2), Cycles 0x17 (1 % of a cycle), TTE 0x11 / TTF 0x20
+ *     (5.625 s), Status 0x00 bit 3 = battery absent;
+ *   charger 0x69 (drivers/battery_v2/max77705_charger.c): CHG_INT_OK 0xB2
+ *     bit 6 = CHGIN valid, CHG_DETAILS_01 0xB4 low nibble (0-2 charging, 3-4
+ *     done, 8 off, 0xB watchdog expired), CNFG_00 0xB7 mode (bit 0 charger,
+ *     bit 2 buck, bit 4 watchdog), CNFG_09 0xC0 input limit and CHG_EN.
+ * Samsung's Linux (TWRP runs it too) leaves the charger OFF on shutdown
+ * (max77705_charger_shutdown: CNFG_00 = 0x04 buck only, CNFG_09 = 0x0F =
+ * 400 mA input, CHG_EN 0). Whether ABL turns it back on is logged at boot.
+ * sysctl kern.sweet_charge=1 turns charging on the way Linux does
+ * (max77705_set_charger_state: CNFG_00 bit 0, with CHG_EN), only with a
+ * sane float voltage (CNFG_04 <= 4.35 V) and the cell below 4.30 V, and
+ * turns it off again on its own at 4.36 V or when the gauge stops answering;
+ * 2 turns it off. Nobody writes the charger otherwise.
  */
